# Ignore directory *contents*, not the directories themselves, so the .gitkeep
# placeholders survive. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent dir is excluded.
data/raw/*
data/interim/*
data/processed/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep
!data/interim/.gitkeep
!data/processed/.gitkeep

# The generated reports, though. They are 114 kB of markdown against 13 MB of
# built HTML and 10 MB GeoTIFFs, and they are the evidence for every claim the
# README and FINDINGS make - blocked AUC, the null control, the record counts,
# the Boyce diagnosis. A project whose selling point is honest validation that
# ships none of its validation output is asking to be taken on trust.
!data/processed/*.md
!data/processed/runs/
!data/processed/runs/*.md

# And the two build inputs, so a clone can actually build the app.
#
# Generated files in source control is normally a smell. The alternative here
# is worse: `payload.js` is the model surface, and regenerating it means 31 GB
# of rasters and an overnight pipeline run. A repository that cannot build its
# own product on a second machine is not much of a repository, and a second
# machine is the reason this one was pushed.
#
# The cost is honest: payload.js is 3.8 MB of base64, so every refit adds that
# much to history for good. Refits happen a handful of times a season, so this
# is tens of megabytes over years - cheap against not being able to build.
#
# conditions.json is deliberately NOT here. It changes every day, the build
# works without it, and `fetch_recent_weather.py --if-new` takes two minutes.
# Committing a file that changes daily across two machines is a conflict
# generator and nothing else.
!data/processed/web_preview/
!data/processed/web_preview/payload.js
!data/interim/trails.json

# Re-close the door. Un-ignoring the directory above also exposed the built
# 13.4 MB single-file interface that lives beside the payload, which is exactly
# the sort of thing these rules exist to keep out. Only the payload is wanted.
data/processed/web_preview/*.html
data/processed/web_preview/*.png

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Supabase project URL and anon key. The anon key is not a secret - RLS is what
# protects the rows - but the file is per-developer, so it stays out of git.
# config/supabase.example.json is committed instead.
config/supabase.json

# Build output. dist/index.html has the Supabase project URL and anon key baked
# into it. The anon key is not a secret - row-level security is what protects
# the rows - but a built artefact has no business in source control, and the
# habit of committing one is how the *next* key ends up public.
dist/
dist_norge/

# Wrangler's local state. `wrangler-account.json` holds the Cloudflare account
# id and the account holder's email address; neither belongs in a public repo.
.wrangler/
node_modules/

# Anything that looks like a private key, wherever it lands. The dev
# certificate under data/interim/ is already covered by the data rules above;
# this is the backstop for the one that is not.
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
